52479	52697	52476	52477	Mr. Hatfield	415	“ As you are well aware , the poor youth never recovered the shock which he sustained on learning that he owed his being to that dreadful man -- Benjamin Bones ; and the horrible way in which that remorseless wretch died , augmented the weight of the fearful blow caused by that discovery . Jacob scarcely ever rallied -- scarcely ever held up his head afterwards : the only gleam of happiness which he knew was afforded by the good tidings that we received relative to the Bunces -- and even that was insufficient to sustain his drooping spirit . He languished away -- for six years he pined in sorrow , accessible to no consolation that travelling , change of scenery , or our attentions could impart . It was several years before the Great Revolution , which , conducted by Richard Markham , gave freedom to Castelcicala and raised up that hero to a princely rank,--it was some years before this glorious era , that Jacob Smith -- for he always retained that name -- breathed his last . We buried him in a picturesque cemetery on the banks of the river Ferretti ; and a cross -- according to the custom of that Catholic country -- was placed to mark his last home . ”
